---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
---
<BaseLayout
  title="Rawan Aladdin — For Monks"
  description="A targeted mini-portfolio: brand systems carried across static, motion, print and environment, by Rawan Aladdin, for the Monks Designer, Social & Brand role."
  bodyClass="mk-pitch"
  canonical="https://rawanaladdin.com/monks"
  noindex
>
  <Fragment slot="head">
    <link rel="stylesheet" href="/css/monks.css" />
  </Fragment>

  <main>

    <!-- ═══════════════════════════════ HERO ═══════════════════════════════ -->
    <section id="hero" class="mk-section" style="min-height: 100vh; justify-content: flex-end; padding-bottom: 8rem;">
      <p class="mk-hero-label">For Monks — Designer, Social &amp; Brand — August 2026</p>
      <h1 class="mk-hero-h1">Hi Monks,<br>I'm Rawan.</h1>
      <p class="mk-hero-sub">
        I write the system down before I make the assets — the ratios, the type
        scale, the things never to do — because that document is what lets the
        twentieth thing go out as fast as the first and still read as the same
        brand. Then I go and make all twenty.
      </p>
      <div class="mk-hero-links">
        <a href="mailto:hello@rawanaladdin.com" class="main-link">hello@rawanaladdin.com &#8599;</a>
        <a href="/projects.html" class="main-link">full portfolio &#8599;</a>
      </div>
    </section>

    <div class="mk-divider"></div>

    <!-- ═══════════════════════ YOUR POSTING, ANSWERED ═══════════════════════ -->
    <section id="map" class="mk-section">
      <p class="mk-section-label">Your posting, answered</p>
      <h2 class="mk-h2">What you asked for,<br>and what I have.</h2>
      <p class="mk-lede">
        Eleven things in your essentials list. Here they are grouped into seven, with
        the specific work that answers each one. The two I can't answer are at the bottom
        of this page, where you can find them without looking.
      </p>

      <div class="mk-map">

        <div class="mk-map-row">
          <p class="mk-map-k">Works inside an<br>existing brand system</p>
          <p class="mk-map-v">
            I've been on both ends of that. On <strong>Feeds</strong> I wrote the system
            somebody else had to build inside, which is the fastest way I know to learn
            what a rule costs the person following it. Then I went into his repository and
            applied it myself, through his code review, which is where the naming of things
            got settled in both directions.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="mk-map-row">
          <p class="mk-map-k">Builds scalable<br>systems</p>
          <p class="mk-map-v">
            Eleven pages of guidelines: a five-step type scale, fifteen icons drawn to one
            grammar, five colours each carrying the share of a page it should occupy, four
            explicit don'ts and an AA contrast floor. Written for one reader, in a hurry.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="mk-map-row">
          <p class="mk-map-k">Static, motion<br>and video</p>
          <p class="mk-map-v">
            On <strong>Feeds</strong>: one identity through a product interface, a digest
            email, a marketing site, printed runners and stickers, and two looping reels,
            inside a single launch. On <strong>SyriaTek</strong>: two vertical 1080 × 1920
            videos I filmed, cut and subtitled in two languages, from twenty-one clips of
            original footage.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="mk-map-row">
          <p class="mk-map-k">Platform-native,<br>premium and lo-fi</p>
          <p class="mk-map-v">
            The Feeds launch is the premium end — printed, specified, a contrast floor.
            <strong>SyriaTek</strong> is the other end and was meant to be: presenter to
            camera, auto-captions, footage shot on a phone-sized budget on a street in
            Damascus, cut to be watched with the sound off on a commute. The identity was
            drawn separately for a circular profile crop, a wide cover, and an animated
            opening.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="mk-map-row">
          <p class="mk-map-k">Typography, layout,<br>image selection</p>
          <p class="mk-map-v">
            A type scale specified step by step and then set — 108px display down to a 21px
            caption, each step named for what it is for. On the <strong>SACABC</strong>
            redesign the art direction was the argument: a pattern-led hero pulled from the
            community's own visual language, carried through the web and social graphics as
            one system.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="mk-map-row">
          <p class="mk-map-k">Retail, culture,<br>audience</p>
          <p class="mk-map-v">
            <strong>SyriaTek</strong> is an Arabic-language series I built for an audience
            I belong to, where the dialect, the terminology and the tone were the design
            problem. Before it: a cultural nonprofit's site and social graphics at
            <strong>SACABC</strong>, and an academic year directing photo and video at the
            <strong>Muslim Students' Association</strong> at U of T. I'm bilingual in Arabic
            and English.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="mk-map-row">
          <p class="mk-map-k">Moves quickly<br>without losing craft</p>
          <p class="mk-map-v">
            That year of coverage ran on somebody else's event schedule, shot, cut and
            turned around. A capstone week where the booth, the reels, the print and the live
            site all landed at once. And on SyriaTek, honestly, a production approach that
            was too polished to sustain — which is the lesson I actually took from it.
          </p>
        </div>

      </div>
    </section>

    <div class="mk-divider"></div>

    <!-- ═══════════════════════ PROJECT 01 — THE SYSTEM ═══════════════════════ -->
    <section id="feeds-system" class="mk-section">
      <p class="mk-section-label">Project 01 — The system</p>
      <div class="mk-project-head">
        <h2 class="mk-project-title">Feeds</h2>
        <span class="mk-meta">Brand Identity · Launch · 2026</span>
      </div>
      <p class="mk-lede">
        A self-hosted reader and digest engine for security analysts. The client was the
        engineer building it, and the brief was a product with no visual personality at all.
        I authored the identity and the system underneath it, then carried it onto every
        surface the launch needed.
      </p>

      <div class="mk-tags">
        <span class="mk-tag">Brand Identity</span>
        <span class="mk-tag">Design System</span>
        <span class="mk-tag">Guidelines</span>
        <span class="mk-tag">Art Direction</span>
        <span class="mk-tag">Print</span>
        <span class="mk-tag">Motion</span>
      </div>

      <div class="mk-spec">
        <div>
          <div class="mk-spec-n">11</div>
          <div class="mk-spec-l">Pages of<br>guidelines</div>
        </div>
        <div>
          <div class="mk-spec-n">5</div>
          <div class="mk-spec-l">Type steps,<br>each specified</div>
        </div>
        <div>
          <div class="mk-spec-n">15</div>
          <div class="mk-spec-l">Custom icons,<br>one grammar</div>
        </div>
        <div>
          <div class="mk-spec-n">50/28/15/7</div>
          <div class="mk-spec-l">Palette usage,<br>as a ratio</div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="mk-block">
        <p class="mk-block-n">01 — The mark</p>
        <h3>A four-point spark, held between two curved strokes.</h3>
        <p>
          The wordmark is lowercase and never capitalises, in Bricolage Grotesque. Three
          approved lockups — on paper, on ink, on sage — and three supporting textures,
          one of which became the marketing site's whole ground.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="mk-block">
        <p class="mk-block-n">02 — Specified down to the ratio</p>
        <h3>Every rule is something you can check your own work against.</h3>
        <p>
          Five colours, each carrying the proportion of a page it should occupy — paper 50%,
          ink 28%, sage 15%, the tints the remaining 7%. A five-step type scale from a 108px
          display to a 21px caption, each step named for what it is for. Fifteen custom icons
          sharing one grammar: 2px strokes, no fill, rounded joins, one grid, with a rule
          that new icons join the family before anyone reaches for a stock set.
        </p>
        <p>
          Four explicit don'ts: never stretch the mark, never recolour it outside the
          palette, never rotate it, never place it where contrast drops below AA.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="mk-decision">
        <p class="mk-decision-k">Who the document was written for</p>
        <p>
          One reader: the developer building the product, who would open it while he was
          busy with something else. That decides what goes in. Everything in there is a
          ratio, a pixel value, or a thing never to do — something he can hold his own work
          up against in ten seconds. I left the adjectives out.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="mk-media-grid">
        <div class="mk-media">
          <img src="/feeds/guidelines-05.webp" alt="Page 5 of the Feeds brand guidelines — Logo Usage. Three approved lockup combinations on paper, ink and sage, above four don'ts: never stretch, never recolour outside the palette, never place where contrast drops below AA, never rotate." loading="lazy" />
        </div>
        <div class="mk-media">
          <img src="/feeds/guidelines-06.webp" alt="Page 6 of the Feeds brand guidelines — Color Palette. Five swatches with hex and RGB values and a stated role for each, above a usage-ratio bar reading paper 50 percent, ink 28 percent, sage 15 percent, sage dark and light 7 percent." loading="lazy" />
        </div>
        <div class="mk-media">
          <img src="/feeds/guidelines-07.webp" alt="Page 7 of the Feeds brand guidelines — Typography. A five-step type scale in Bricolage Grotesque from 108px display to 21px caption, each with weight and purpose, plus the full weight range and the variable font's axes." loading="lazy" />
        </div>
        <div class="mk-media">
          <img src="/feeds/guidelines-08.webp" alt="Page 8 of the Feeds brand guidelines — Iconography. Fifteen custom icons on a consistent grid, from Feed and AI to Phishing and Monitoring, all drawn with 2px strokes, no fill and rounded joins." loading="lazy" />
        </div>
      </div>
      <p class="mk-caption">Four of the eleven pages: logo usage and the don'ts, the palette with its ratios, the type scale, and the icon set.</p>
    </section>

    <div class="mk-divider"></div>

    <!-- ═══════════════════════ THE FORMAT RAIL ═══════════════════════ -->
    <section id="surfaces" class="mk-section">
      <p class="mk-section-label">Project 01, continued — The surfaces</p>
      <h2 class="mk-h2">One system,<br>six formats.</h2>
      <p class="mk-lede">
        This is the part I'd point at for a social brief. The system had to hold its
        shape in a browser at 13px, on a printed runner at three metres, and in a reel
        looping on a screen behind it — inside the same week, for the same launch.
      </p>

      <div class="mk-formats">

        <article class="mk-format">
          <p class="mk-format-k">01 — Product UI</p>
          <div class="mk-media">
            <img src="/feeds/feeds-ui-smartinbox.webp" alt="The Feeds Smart Inbox after the brand refresh — saved-filter results with inline CVE, IOC and malware chips, in the ink-on-paper palette." loading="lazy" />
          </div>
          <p class="mk-format-v">
            The interface and the icon set, applied in CSS and JavaScript inside the
            engineer's repository and reviewed like anyone else's work. Some UX calls came
            out of that back-and-forth.
          </p>
        </article>

        <article class="mk-format">
          <p class="mk-format-k">02 — Marketing site</p>
          <div class="mk-media">
            <img src="/feeds/feeds-site-hero.webp" alt="The Feeds marketing site hero — the lowercase wordmark and spark mark on the topographic texture, the tagline 'Your feeds. Your disk. Your loop.', and three pill chips reading Local-first, Hackable, Optional AI." loading="lazy" />
          </div>
          <p class="mk-format-v">
            The same texture from the brand kit as the whole ground, three claims as chips,
            and the product's real numbers underneath them. Rebranded end to end.
          </p>
        </article>

        <article class="mk-format">
          <p class="mk-format-k">03 — Motion</p>
          <div class="mk-media">
            <video
              src="/feeds/feeds-booth-reel.mp4"
              poster="/feeds/feeds-booth-reel-poster.jpg"
              width="1280" height="720"
              autoplay muted loop playsinline preload="metadata"
              aria-label="The Feeds booth reel — the brand identity animating through mark, palette and product screens on a loop.">
            </video>
          </div>
          <p class="mk-format-v">
            Two reels cut for the launch — one on the identity, one on the product — built
            to loop on the screens behind the table without a soundtrack or a viewer who
            arrived at the beginning.
          </p>
        </article>

        <article class="mk-format">
          <p class="mk-format-k">04 — Print &amp; environment</p>
          <div class="mk-media">
            <img class="mk-crop-low" src="/feeds/feeds-booth.webp" alt="The Feeds booth during the showcase — two printed table runners carrying the wordmark and tagline, die-cut mark stickers in four colourways laid across the table, a laptop looping the identity reel, and the live product site on the screen behind." loading="lazy" />
          </div>
          <p class="mk-format-v">
            Two printed table runners and die-cut mark stickers in four colourways, all
            pulled from the same brand kit. Nothing was redrawn for print.
          </p>
        </article>

      </div>

      <p class="mk-note">
        The fifth and sixth surfaces have no photograph worth showing: the digest email,
        reskinned to the same system, and the README and app screenshots that went out with
        the release. Worth being precise about ownership on all of it — the Go engine, the
        architecture and the core product logic are the engineer's. The identity, the
        guidelines, the applied front-end, the email, the marketing site and the launch are
        mine. The engine is his; everything you can see is mine.
      </p>

      <div class="mk-cta-links">
        <a href="/feeds.html" class="main-link">read the full case study &#8599;</a>
      </div>
    </section>

    <div class="mk-divider"></div>

    <!-- ═══════════════════════ PROJECT 02 — SYRIATEK ═══════════════════════ -->
    <section id="syriatek" class="mk-section">
      <p class="mk-section-label">Project 02 — The feed</p>
      <div class="mk-project-head">
        <h2 class="mk-project-title">SyriaTek</h2>
        <span class="mk-meta">Brand Identity · Social · Jun – Aug 2026</span>
      </div>
      <p class="mk-lede">
        An Arabic-language technology series for Syrians inside Syria and across the
        diaspora — people who use a smartphone every day and were never handed the rest of
        it. I made the identity, wrote the production system, filmed it, cut it, published
        it, and read the numbers afterwards. This is the piece where the system habit met
        an actual feed.
      </p>

      <div class="mk-tags">
        <span class="mk-tag">Brand Identity</span>
        <span class="mk-tag">Bilingual</span>
        <span class="mk-tag">Social</span>
        <span class="mk-tag">Short-form Video</span>
        <span class="mk-tag">Art Direction</span>
        <span class="mk-tag">Editing</span>
      </div>

      <div class="mk-stats">
        <div>
          <div class="mk-stat-n">3,823</div>
          <div class="mk-stat-l">Views, first<br>15 days</div>
        </div>
        <div>
          <div class="mk-stat-n">1,556</div>
          <div class="mk-stat-l">Unique<br>viewers</div>
        </div>
        <div>
          <div class="mk-stat-n">92%</div>
          <div class="mk-stat-l">Of reach from<br>non-followers</div>
        </div>
        <div>
          <div class="mk-stat-n">21 / 100</div>
          <div class="mk-stat-l">Followers against<br>my own goal</div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="mk-block">
        <p class="mk-block-n">01 — The identity</p>
        <h3>Two scripts that had to sit together.</h3>
        <p>
          An Arabic wordmark and a Latin one, drawn so neither reads as a translation of the
          other — the same open counters and the same green accent carried across both
          alphabets. A circuit trace rising into a chevron does the rest of the work, and the
          Arabic line under it reads “teaching technology, building the future, Syria
          forward.”
        </p>
        <p>
          Then it was cut for where it actually lives: a circular profile mark that survives
          the crop, a Facebook cover at a completely different ratio, and an animated wordmark
          for the openings. Same system, four container shapes, which is the same problem as
          the section above with a different client.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="mk-formats">

        <article class="mk-format">
          <p class="mk-format-k">01 — Profile mark</p>
          <div class="mk-media">
            <img src="/syriatek/syriatek-mark.webp" alt="The SyriaTek profile mark — a dark green processor chip carrying three stars, circuit traces running out to the edges, with the SyriaTek wordmark in Latin and Arabic beneath, on a sand-coloured circle." loading="lazy" />
          </div>
          <p class="mk-format-v">
            Drawn inside the circle so nothing important sits where the crop bites.
          </p>
        </article>

        <article class="mk-format">
          <p class="mk-format-k">02 — Cover</p>
          <div class="mk-media">
            <img src="/syriatek/syriatek-cover.webp" alt="The SyriaTek Facebook cover — the Arabic and Latin wordmarks centred above a rising chevron of circuit traces, with the Arabic tagline beneath in green." loading="lazy" />
          </div>
          <p class="mk-format-v">
            The same chevron rebuilt for a wide banner, where the circle version would have
            collapsed.
          </p>
        </article>

        <article class="mk-format">
          <p class="mk-format-k">03 — The account</p>
          <div class="mk-media">
            <img src="/syriatek/syriatek-profile.webp" alt="The live @syria.tek Instagram profile — bio in Arabic and English, a link to the Facebook page, and the two published Reels shown as covers in the grid." loading="lazy" />
          </div>
          <p class="mk-format-v">
            Live at <strong>@syria.tek</strong>, with the Facebook page linked from the bio.
          </p>
        </article>

        <article class="mk-format">
          <p class="mk-format-k">04 — The videos</p>
          <div class="mk-media">
            <img class="mk-contain" src="/syriatek/syriatek-frame-1.webp" alt="A frame from the second SyriaTek pilot video — vertical presenter footage with an Arabic subtitle line and its English translation stacked beneath it." loading="lazy" />
          </div>
          <p class="mk-format-v">
            Vertical at 1080 × 1920, presenter-led, Arabic subtitles with English stacked
            under them so one cut serves both audiences.
          </p>
        </article>

      </div>

      <div class="mk-block">
        <p class="mk-block-n">02 — The production system</p>
        <h3>A new video starts from rules already written down.</h3>
        <p>
          I wrote a reusable guide — the Script Studio — so a script begins from decisions
          already made: the mission, the audience, the Syrian Arabic language approach,
          a 30 to 45 second format, a four-part script structure, B-roll direction, and the
          quality checks a draft has to pass before it gets made. Does the hook work. Does the
          language sound like speech. Does the video teach one idea. Is the audience
          respected. Should the viewer feel more capable at the end.
        </p>
        <p>
          It is the same instinct as the eleven pages above, aimed at output. The reason I
          would bring it up in a conversation about volume is that it is the only thing I
          have found that makes the fifth video cost less than the first.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="mk-block">
        <p class="mk-block-n">03 — Shot, cut and shipped</p>
        <h3>Twenty-one original clips, filmed on the street in Syria.</h3>
        <p>
          Streets, markets, vendors, cafés, storefronts, traffic — about six minutes and
          fifty seconds of original footage, recorded over three days at the end of June, so
          the series would look like the place it is speaking to. Presenter footage on a DSLR
          with a USB microphone. Both pilots cut in CapCut: my sequencing, my pacing, my
          music, my caption review, my exports.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="mk-media-grid mk-even">
        <div>
          <div class="mk-media">
            <img class="mk-crop-mid" src="/syriatek/syriatek-broll-2.webp" alt="A frame of original SyriaTek B-roll — a Syrian street in the late afternoon, shopfronts along one side and people walking past parked cars." loading="lazy" />
          </div>
          <p class="mk-caption">One of twenty-one original clips, shot on the street in Syria.</p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <div class="mk-media">
            <img src="/syriatek/syriatek-timeline.webp" alt="The CapCut timeline for the first pilot video — presenter footage, B-roll, Arabic and English caption tracks, music and audio layers stacked across the edit." loading="lazy" />
          </div>
          <p class="mk-caption">The CapCut timeline: presenter footage, B-roll, two caption tracks, music.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="mk-decision">
        <p class="mk-decision-k">The call I would defend</p>
        <p>
          Writing the next script, a practical lesson on getting online, I took out the word
          for router. I was not certain my audience used it, so the line became the Wi-Fi
          device at home or at work. I threw out a draft that told viewers to go to a shop for
          help and taught them to do it themselves, because the whole promise of the series is
          that you can learn this. And I changed موصول to متصل, because one of them is what
          people actually say out loud. None of that shows in the finished video. All of it is
          the reason it lands.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="mk-block">
        <p class="mk-block-n">04 — The numbers, read honestly</p>
        <h3>Strong discovery, weak conversion.</h3>
        <p>
          Fifteen days after launch: 3,823 views, 1,556 unique viewers, 157 interactions, 95
          accounts engaged, all delivery through Reels. Only 7.6% of views came from people
          already following, so the reach was almost entirely discovery. I had set myself a
          hundred followers by the end of the term and I finished on twenty-one.
        </p>
        <p>
          The cause is not mysterious. Two videos cannot build the trust that makes somebody
          follow an account, and both of them were introductions — they established the tone
          and the promise and never got to the first practical lesson, which is the thing
          people would have come back for. The next moves I would make are the ones that
          follow from that: batch the scripts, keep one filming setup, publish the useful
          video early, and measure retention and saves at a fixed interval after each post.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="mk-honest">
        <p class="mk-honest-k">Where AI was and was not used</p>
        <p>
          Two tools, and worth being exact about both. <strong>ChatGPT</strong> for clarifying
          the concept, drafting and revising Syrian Arabic scripts, shot lists, captions, and
          for building the Script Studio guide with me. <strong>CapCut's AI features</strong>
          for automatic captions, auto-reframe, colour consistency and clarity, plus audio
          cleanup on the first video.
        </p>
        <p>
          Nothing visual was generated. The identity is drawn, the footage is filmed, and the
          cuts, pacing, music, caption corrections and final exports are decisions I made. The
          useful surprise was that building a reusable guide beat asking for individual
          scripts — and that a fast bad draft is worth having, because hearing what is wrong
          in Arabic is quicker than starting the sentence myself.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="mk-cta-links">
        <a href="https://www.instagram.com/syria.tek/" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="main-link">see the account &#8599;</a>
      </div>

      <p class="mk-footnote">
        SyriaTek began as a term project for CREA 4055 at BCIT. It is a real public pilot with
        a real audience, and it is also two videos — I would rather you had both of those facts.
      </p>
    </section>

    <div class="mk-divider"></div>

    <!-- ═══════════════════════ MOTION ═══════════════════════ -->
    <section id="motion" class="mk-section">
      <p class="mk-section-label">Motion</p>
      <h2 class="mk-h2">Where the<br>motion comes from.</h2>
      <p class="mk-lede">
        Your essentials ask for motion instincts and your nice-to-haves ask for After
        Effects, so here is exactly what that is for me, tool by tool.
      </p>

      <ul class="mk-list">
        <li>
          <strong>Premiere Pro</strong> — an academic year of photo and video post-production
          at the Muslim Students' Association, cutting event coverage on a turnaround.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>After Effects</strong> — social and asset work, and school projects. Real
          hours in it, and no shipped client piece I'd walk you through frame by frame.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Lottie and GSAP</strong> — motion that has to live in a browser and survive
          a reduced-motion setting, on this site and in product work.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>CapCut</strong> — both SyriaTek pilots, edited end to end: sequencing,
          pacing, music, two caption tracks, 9:16 formatting and the final exports.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>The reels</strong> — two loops cut for the Feeds launch booth, and the
          animated SyriaTek wordmark.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <div class="mk-media mk-media-narrow">
        <video
          src="/feeds/feeds-ripple.mp4"
          autoplay muted loop playsinline preload="metadata"
          aria-label="A looping ripple animation built from the Feeds topographic texture.">
        </video>
      </div>
      <p class="mk-caption">The topographic texture from the brand kit, animated into a loop.</p>
    </section>

    <div class="mk-divider"></div>

